Britain's Royal Navy is sounding out the defense industry ahead of kicking off a procurement process for the Type 93, a large uncrewed submarine that will form part of the force’s Hybrid Navy vision. The Ministry of Defence (MoD) issued a preliminary market engagement notice inviting organizations to register their interest in the Type 93 and the AUKUS Signature Payload Program, which aims to develop weapons and other systems for the UK’s uncrewed submarine and those of partner nations. (AUKUS, is a trilateral security partnership between Australia, the UK and US partly focused on submarine technology.

) The purpose of the notice is to gather a comprehensive list of interested suppliers for Extra-Large Uncrewed Underwater Vehicle (XLUUV) platforms and the wider ecosystem: payload providers, suppliers of relevant tech and software including systems for autonomous operations, AI and mission management systems. Integrators and firms to provide through-life support are also sought. The move shows that the Royal Navy is trying to move forward and bring into service an uncrewed combat submarine capable of operating independently, or alongside crewed vessels, to defend the UK’s home waters and infrastructure such as undersea cables.

According to the notice, the Royal Navy is initially considering subs approximately 12 meters (40 ft) in length and 2 meters (6. 5 ft) in diameter as potential candidates for Type 93, although organisations with existing systems of other dimensions are also encouraged to register. It is perhaps no coincidence that two existing uncrewed submarine prototypes roughly fit these dimensions; the XV Excalibur, which is a large submarine drone the Royal Navy has been using in seagoing trials; and the BAE Systems Herne.
